
          INSTALL of  APRX 2


Instructions for how to build Debian apt packages are provided
at the end of this file


A rough-cut version of the installation instructions

  0) Prerequisites:
      Does not need anything beside standard libc!
      (In particular the Linux version does not need
       libax25 / libax25-dev !)

  1) Start with  ./configure  --parameters
     For a small memory system without writable /tmp
     you have to use  --with-embedded   option.

  2) Cleanliness is good start:

         $ make clean

  3) Compile the thing:

         $ make

  4) There is automatic "install" as:

         # make install

      with several presumptions about directories fixed
      into the Makefile (possibly some adjustments are
      required, depending upon your environment.)

  5) Edit the configuration file to match your system:

        # $EDITOR /etc/aprx.conf

     See the  aprx(8) man-page for more info (man 8 aprx)

  6) Program startup scripts ("init-scripts") exist for a
     couple system environments, others may need manual
     adapting.

For Debian users wanting to compile themselves instead of using
precompiled binaries:

	# aptitude install git build-essential debhelper libssl-dev
	$ git clone https://github.com/PhirePhly/aprx.git
	$ cd aprx
	$ ./configure
	$ make make-deb
	# dpkg -i ../aprx_....deb


Debian 13 (trixie) and APRS LoRa KISS devices  -- EA7JRS fork
---------------------------------------------------------------

This fork only fixes the build on current compilers and packaging
tools; the KISS code and everything else is upstream aprx (2.9.1 plus
the later upstream commits).

  - GCC 10 and later (Debian 13 ships GCC 14) failed to link with
    "multiple definition of aprsis_thread / pthr_attrs / now".
  - GCC 14 refused the old K&R definition of aprx_cfmakeraw().
  - The Debian packaging used debhelper compat 5, which current
    debhelper refuses; it now uses compat 13.

1. Give the LoRa device a stable name (optional but recommended).
   Find its USB ids with:

	$ lsusb

   and create /etc/udev/rules.d/99-lora-aprs.rules with:

	SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="YOURidVendor", ATTRS{idProduct}=="YOURidProduct", SYMLINK+="ttyLoraAPRS", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"

	$ sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger

2. Build and install as a Debian package (recommended: it brings the
   init script, logrotate and apparmor files, and apt knows about it):

	$ sudo apt install build-essential debhelper fakeroot git
	$ git clone https://github.com/EA7JRS/aprx.git
	$ cd aprx
	$ ./configure
	$ make make-deb
	$ sudo dpkg -i ../aprx_2.9.1+ea7jrs1_*.deb

   Or build and install directly, without a package:

	$ ./configure
	$ make
	$ sudo make install

3. Point aprx to the device in /etc/aprx.conf, inside an <interface>
   block:

	serial-device /dev/ttyLoraAPRS 115200 8n1 KISS

   The device must speak standard KISS with binary AX.25 frames (as the
   APRS LoRa+ tracker and the CA2RXU firmwares do in KISS mode), and
   nothing else on that serial port: debug or log output mixed into
   the same port corrupts the KISS stream. Disable it in the firmware.
